0. Clone access-UK repo from github inside WSL:
   - Create an SSH key (ed25519 is the recommended key type):
     ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -a 100
   - Copy the public key:
     cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
   - Add it in github.com -> Settings -> SSH and GPG keys.
   - Clone the repo:
     git clone git@github.com:shaakaud/access-UK.git
   - Keep repo under Linux filesystem (example: ~/access-UK).

0.5 Make Agents Globally Available (Optional but Recommended):
   - VS Code agents in ~/access-UK/agents will guide your WSL setup.
   - To make these agents available in any VS Code workspace:
     mkdir -p ~/.vscode
     ln -s ~/access-UK/agents ~/.vscode/agents
   - Verify the symlink:
     ls -la ~/.vscode/agents/
     # Should show: agents -> /home/youruser/access-UK/agents
   - Now invoke @wsl_dev_setup from ANY VS Code workspace.
   - Logs saved to: ~/access-UK/.wsl_setup_logs/wsl_setup.<timestamp>.log

1. Install base dev tools required by other steps:
   - `curl`, `wget`, `unzip`, `tar`, `ca-certificates`:
     download and extract tools safely.
   - `build-essential`, `make`, `pkg-config`: compile native tools.
   - `jq`: JSON processor. `yq`: YAML processor.
   - Install:
     sudo apt update
     sudo apt install -y curl wget unzip tar ca-certificates
     sudo apt install -y build-essential make pkg-config jq yq

2. Set up shell dotfiles:
   - ⏭️ SKIP THIS STEP: This setup uses zsh as primary shell (STEP 3 installs it).
   - Bash files are not needed for zsh-only workflow.
   - No need to symlink bash files (~/.bashrc, ~/.bashrc.local, etc.).

3. Install zsh and Oh-My-Zsh:
   - zsh: shell with better completion and prompt support.
   - ⚠️ BACKUP: Create backups of existing dotfiles:
     cp ~/.zshrc ~/.zshrc.bak.$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S) 2>/dev/null || true
     cp ~/.zshrc.local ~/.zshrc.local.bak.$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S) 2>/dev/null || true
   - Install:
     sudo apt update
     sudo apt install -y zsh
   - Link your zsh files:
     ln -sfn ~/access-UK/zsh/zshrc ~/.zshrc
     ln -sfn ~/access-UK/zsh/zshrc.local ~/.zshrc.local
   - Install oh-my-zsh framework (use unattended mode):
     sh -c "$(curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ohmyzsh/ohmyzsh/\
master/tools/install.sh)" "" --unattended || true
   - Install zsh git prompt dependency:
     git clone https://github.com/shaakaud/zsh-git-prompt.git \
~/access-UK/zsh/zsh-git-prompt
   - Ensure oh-my-zsh is enabled in your linked `~/.zshrc`:
     - Open `~/.zshrc`
     - Ensure this line is NOT commented:
       source $ZSH/oh-my-zsh.sh
   - Set zsh as login shell:
     chsh -s /usr/bin/zsh
   - Start a new shell to validate:
     exec zsh
   - If you see this error:
     `/home/<user>/access-UK/zsh/zsh-git-prompt/zshrc.sh: no such file`
     clone the repo line above again and then run `exec zsh`.

4. Install DejaVu Font (recommended default):
   - DejaVu Sans Mono is stable, simple, and works everywhere.
   
   **Step 1: Install on Windows Side (REQUIRED for Windows Terminal):**
   
   - Download DejaVu ZIP from: https://github.com/dejavu-fonts/dejavu/releases
   - Extract the ZIP file (e.g., to ~/Downloads/dejavu-fonts/)
   - Open Windows PowerShell as Administrator:
     # Navigate to extracted fonts folder
     cd "$env:USERPROFILE\Downloads\dejavu-fonts\ttf"
     
     # Copy all .ttf files to Windows Fonts folder
     Copy-Item *.ttf "C:\Windows\Fonts\"
     
   - Verify in Windows: Settings → Fonts → Search "DejaVu" (should see DejaVu Sans Mono)
   
   **Step 2: Install on WSL Side (for consistency):**
   
   - Install DejaVu fonts in WSL:
     sudo apt update
     sudo apt install -y fonts-dejavu fonts-dejavu-core
   - Verify:
     fc-list | grep -i dejavu
   
   **Step 3: Configure Windows Terminal:**
   
   - Open Windows Terminal settings.json (Ctrl+,)
   - Find the Ubuntu profile and add/update:
     "fontFace": "DejaVu Sans Mono"
   - Restart Windows Terminal
   - Ubuntu app should now display DejaVu Sans Mono font

   ⚠️ OPTIONAL ALTERNATIVE: Powerlevel9k Theme
   
   If you prefer fancy prompt with git status icons (Nerd Font required):

   **Powerlevel9k Pros:**
   - Beautiful multi-line prompt with git status
   - Shows branch, commit status, uncommitted changes
   - Customizable colors and icons
   - Great for visual developers

   **Powerlevel9k Cons:**
   - Requires Nerd Font (CaskaydiaCove) on Windows Terminal
   - Slower prompt rendering (noticeable on large repos)
   - More dependencies (external font installation)
   - Harder to troubleshoot if font config issues arise

   **DejaVu Font Pros:**
   - Simple, clean, professional look
   - No special font installation needed
   - Faster prompt rendering
   - Works consistently across all terminals

   **DejaVu Font Cons:**
   - Less visual flair than Nerd Fonts
   - No git icons in prompt

   To install powerlevel9k instead, run:
     git clone https://github.com/Powerlevel9k/powerlevel9k.git \
~/.oh-my-zsh/custom/themes/powerlevel9k

   Then uncomment `ZSH_THEME="powerlevel9k/powerlevel9k"` in ~/.zshrc
   (Note: Requires CaskaydiaCove Nerd Font on Windows Terminal)

5. Configure general dotfiles (on need basis):
   - Link the common files:
     ln -sfn ~/access-UK/others/inputrc ~/.inputrc
     ln -sfn ~/access-UK/gdb/gdbinit ~/.gdbinit

6. Configure git:
   - ⚠️ BACKUP: Create backup of existing dotfile:
     cp ~/.gitconfig ~/.gitconfig.bak.$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S) 2>/dev/null || true
   - Link git files:
     ln -sfn ~/access-UK/git/gitconfig ~/.gitconfig
     ln -sfn ~/access-UK/git/git-prompt.sh ~/.git-prompt.sh
     ln -sfn ~/access-UK/bin/git ~/bin/git
   - Install git (if not already installed):
     sudo apt update
     sudo apt install -y git
   - ⚠️ DO NOT run: git lfs install
     Git LFS is only needed if tracking large binaries (>100MB).
     This repo does NOT use git-lfs. Do not run git lfs install.
   - Recommendation for your usage pattern:
     - Use Ubuntu git by default.
     - Use Git PPA only if you need a specific new feature or fix.
   - Git PPA path (optional):
     sudo add-apt-repository ppa:git-core/ppa
     sudo apt update
     sudo apt install -y git

7. Install tmux:
   - tmux: terminal multiplexer (persistent sessions).
   - ⚠️ BACKUP: Create backup of existing dotfile:
     cp ~/.tmux.conf ~/.tmux.conf.bak.$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S) 2>/dev/null || true
   - Install:
     sudo apt update
     sudo apt install -y tmux
   - Link config:
     ln -sfn ~/access-UK/tmux/tmux.conf ~/.tmux.conf
   - TPM (tmux plugin manager): installs and updates tmux plugins.
     git clone https://github.com/tmux-plugins/tpm ~/.tmux/plugins/tpm
   - Your [tmux/tmux.conf](tmux/tmux.conf) already includes:
     - `tmux-resurrect`: saves/restores tmux sessions, windows, panes.
     - `tmux-continuum`: auto-saves tmux state periodically.
  - No need to add these plugins manually.
  - Install via TPM inside tmux: `prefix + I`.
   - Load config:
     tmux source-file ~/.tmux.conf
   - Plugin install inside tmux:
     prefix + I
   - Expected TPM output after install:
     `TMUX environment reloaded. Done, press ENTER to continue.`
   - Verify plugins are present:
     ls ~/.tmux/plugins
   - Verify resurrect save files (new default path):
     ls ~/.local/share/tmux/resurrect
   - If no save files yet, trigger one manually inside tmux:
     prefix + Ctrl-s
   - Restore manually inside tmux:
     prefix + Ctrl-r

7.5 Set up GitHub Copilot Chat instructions globally:
   - GitHub Copilot Chat automatically loads instructions from `~/.copilot/instructions/`
   - This allows Copilot Chat to enforce coding standards and git workflow rules across ALL projects
   - Create the global instructions directory:
     mkdir -p ~/.copilot/instructions
   - Link the access-UK Copilot instructions:
     ln -sfn ~/access-UK/copilot/instructions.md ~/.copilot/instructions/access-uk.instructions.md
   - Verify the symlink:
     ls -la ~/.copilot/instructions/
     # Should show: access-uk.instructions.md@ -> /home/youruser/access-UK/copilot/instructions.md
   - After setup, restart VS Code for Copilot Chat to reload the instructions
   - Verify instructions are loaded:
     1. Open VS Code with access-UK workspace
     2. Open GitHub Copilot Chat
     3. Type: /instructions (should list "Access-UK Development Standards")
     4. OR check Configure Chat (gear icon) → Instructions tab
   - Instructions include:
     - CRITICAL git workflow rules (never commit/push without permission)
     - Coding standards (80-character line limit)
     - grill-me interview mode for detailed planning
     - Conventions (commit format, file references)

8. Clone Docs-UK (if still used):
   - Reuse the same SSH key from step 0 if already created.
   - If not created yet, generate one:
     ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -a 100
   - Add your key to GitLab:
     cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
     # paste the key in gitlab.com -> Preferences -> SSH Keys
   - Clone in Linux home:
     cd ~
     git clone git@gitlab.com:shaakaud/Docs-UK.git

9. Terminal Colors and Fonts:
   - ⏭️ SKIP THIS STEP - DO NOT RUN
   - Dircolors-solarized is already configured in ~/.zshrc.local
   - Running manual dircolors setup would:
     - Add duplicate lines to zshrc.local
     - Pollute git diff with unnecessary changes
   - Verify already configured:
     grep 'dircolors.ansi-dark' ~/.zshrc.local
   - Optional: Nerd Font setup for Windows Terminal / VS Code terminal:
     - Download from: https://www.nerdfonts.com/font-downloads
     - Pick one font family (example: MesloLGS NF or CaskaydiaCove NF).
     - Extract the downloaded zip to a normal folder first.
     - Install all .ttf files at once (recommended):
       1. Open Windows Settings -> Personalization -> Fonts.
       2. Select all .ttf files in File Explorer.
       3. Drag and drop them into the Fonts install area.
     - If drag-drop is not convenient, use font preview install:
       1. Double-click any .ttf file.
       2. Click Install in the preview window.
       3. Repeat as needed.
     - If right-click does not show "Install for all users", this is okay.
       Use the Settings -> Fonts drag-drop method above.
     - Open Windows Terminal -> Settings -> Ubuntu -> Appearance
     - Set Font face to the CaskaydiaCove Nerd Font.
     - Restart Windows Terminal and run `exec zsh` in WSL.

10. Install fzf:
   - ⏭️ SKIP THIS STEP - DO NOT RUN
   - Zsh already includes fzf via Oh-My-Zsh plugin (enabled by default).
   - Why skip? The fzf installer will pollute bash files despite --no-update-rc flag:
     - Adds sourcing to ~/.bashrc even though you don't use bash
     - Cannot be prevented (installer ignores --no-update-rc)
     - Breaks tool independence and pollutes repo files
   - Verify fzf is already working:
     echo "test" | fzf --filter "test"  # Should return "test"
     bindkey -l | grep fzf  # Should show fzf entries
   - Legacy for bash users (do NOT run for zsh-only setup):
     mkdir -p ~/software
     git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/junegunn/fzf.git ~/software/fzf
     ln -s ~/software/fzf ~/.fzf
     cd ~/software/fzf
     ./install --no-update-rc  ← installer ignores this flag!
   - Continue with step 15 for tmux/dotfile integration used by this repo.

11. Clipboard tools in WSL (if required):
   - For Windows Terminal, extra clipboard packages are usually not required.

11. Clipboard tools in WSL (if required):
   - For Windows Terminal, extra clipboard packages are usually not required.
   - `clip.exe` bridges text from WSL to Windows clipboard.
   - Example:
     echo hello | clip.exe
   - Optional only if you have clipboard issues:
     - `wl-clipboard`: helper for WSLg/Wayland GUI clipboard paths.
       sudo apt update
       sudo apt install -y wl-clipboard
     - `xsel`: legacy X11 clipboard helper.
       sudo apt install -y xsel

12. Install asciidoc if required:
   - ⚠️ SKIP THIS STEP unless you specifically need to convert .asciidoc files
   - asciidoc: text-to-doc converter (not required for standard development)
   - Install only if needed:
     sudo apt update
     sudo apt install -y asciidoc

13. Install mergetool helper used in gitconfig:
   - diffconflicts: custom merge-conflict helper script.
   - ⚠️ BACKUP: Create backup of existing gitconfig if modified:
     cp ~/.gitconfig ~/.gitconfig.bak.$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S) 2>/dev/null || true
   - Your [git/gitconfig](git/gitconfig) already refers to this as merge
     tool (`merge.tool=diffconflicts`).
   - Install:
     cd ~/bin
     if wget -O diffconflicts https://github.com/whiteinge/dotfiles/raw/master/bin/diffconflicts; then
         chmod +x diffconflicts
         echo "✓ diffconflicts installed"
     else
         echo "✗ Failed to download diffconflicts"
         exit 1
     fi

14. Install mosh only if needed:
   - mosh: resilient remote shell over unstable connections.
   - Install:
     sudo apt update
     sudo apt install -y mosh

15. Ensure executable permissions for scripts:
   - Run:
     chmod +x ~/access-UK/bin/*

16. fzf-related helper files:
   - Create files expected by your current shell helpers:
     mkdir -p ~/tmuxbuffer
     touch ~/tmuxbuffer/resultcmdsh
   - inotify-tools: filesystem event watcher.
     sudo apt update
     sudo apt install -y inotify-tools
   - Below info comes from my dot files present in my git repo.
   - No need to do anything for points 1-3.
   - 1. [vim/vimfiles/vundle_vimrc](vim/vimfiles/vundle_vimrc):
        Contains changes to gG/gA to fzf functions and structures
        (right side of vim screen). When selected, it opens the
        function/struct in cscope.
   - 2. [tmux/tmux.conf](tmux/tmux.conf):
        `prefix + y` will pull out ctrl-r kind of window at the bottom
        and shows all the tags from Txt files / my cheat sheets
        (`~/Docs-UK/txtfiles.txt`).
   - 3. [zsh/zshrc.local](zsh/zshrc.local):
        Contains personal configurations, Git settings, and workspace functions:
        - `cmdsh()` and `cmdsh2ndpart()` - fzf-tmux file search for ~/Docs-UK/txtfiles.txt
        - Platform detection for VI, CSCOPE_EDITOR, TERM settings
        - General utilities and aliases (f, m, etc.)
   - If `~/Docs-UK/txtfiles.txt` does not exist, create/populate it
     before expecting `cmdsh()` to return results.

16. noip dynamic DNS (optional):
   - noip: dynamic DNS updater.
   - Use only if you need public DNS updates from home/lab.
   - WSL office/dev usage usually does not require this.

17. Vim legacy plugins (optional):
   - Unite.vim and vimproc are legacy.
   - If you keep them, use your old Vim flow from the original README.

19. Neovim setup (NvChad only):
   - ⚠️ BACKUP: Create backup of existing nvim config:
     cp -r ~/.config/nvim ~/.config/nvim.bak.$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S) 2>/dev/null || true
   - Download in `~/software`, install in `/opt/nvim`, and link binary.
   - Commands:
     mkdir -p ~/software
     cd ~/software
     sudo apt remove -y neovim || true
     sudo apt install -y curl tar jq

     # Get latest Neovim release with error handling
     NVIM_TAG=$(curl -fsSL https://api.github.com/repos/neovim/neovim/releases/latest 2>/dev/null | jq -r '.tag_name' 2>/dev/null)
     if [ -z "$NVIM_TAG" ]; then
         echo "Failed to fetch Neovim version. Using v0.9.0"
         NVIM_TAG="v0.9.0"
     fi
     echo "Installing Neovim: $NVIM_TAG"

     NVIM_URL="https://github.com/neovim/neovim/releases/download/${NVIM_TAG}/nvim-linux-x86_64.tar.gz"
     curl -fLO "$NVIM_URL" || { echo "Failed to download Neovim"; exit 1; }
     tar xzf nvim-linux-x86_64.tar.gz
     sudo rm -rf /opt/nvim
     sudo mv nvim-linux-x86_64 /opt/nvim
     sudo ln -sf /opt/nvim/bin/nvim /usr/local/bin/nvim
     which nvim
     nvim --version | head -n 1

   - Install NvChad (recommended, single path):
     sudo apt install -y ripgrep python3-pip clangd nodejs npm
     sudo npm install -g pyright

     mv ~/.config/nvim ~/.config/oldnvim 2>/dev/null || true
     mkdir -p ~/software/nvimcfg
     git clone https://github.com/shaakaud/starter ~/software/nvimcfg/starter
     ln -sfn ~/software/nvimcfg/starter ~/.config/nvim

   - Open nvim and run:
     :MasonInstallAll

   - lazy.nvim and Mason notes:
     - If using NvChad/starter, lazy.nvim and Mason are already wired.
     - Use `:Lazy` and `:Mason` inside nvim.

19. Install Python base toolchain:
   - Install:
     sudo apt update
     sudo apt install -y python3 python3-pip python3-venv
   - Optional uv (fast Python package/env manager):
     curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh

20. WSLg GUI apps (optional):
   - ⚠️ SKIP THIS STEP - GUI tools are not required for standard development
   - Only install if you specifically need graphical applications in WSL
   - WSLg is Windows Subsystem for Linux GUI support on Windows 11.
   - If needed, ensure WSLg components are updated from PowerShell:
     wsl --update
     wsl --shutdown
   - DO NOT install: gnome-text-editor, x11-apps, nautilus (not needed)

21. Sharing files with Windows:
   - Use \\wsl$ in Windows Explorer.
   - Do not set up Samba inside WSL for normal file sharing.

22. Workspace helper from old README:
   - access-UK/bin has `pwd.sh`.
   - Running the source commands below stores current `pwd` in `$WS`.
   - Alias `base` from `aliases_bash` returns you to `$WS`.
   - `aliases_bash` also uses `$WS` for folder navigation shortcuts.
   - Commands:
     source ~/.zshrc && source ~/bin/pwd.sh

23. DISPLAY/X11 note:
   - On modern WSLg, do not set DISPLAY=localhost:0.0 manually.
   - For old non-WSLg setups only, use an X server workflow.

24. GitHub Copilot CLI (optional):
   - gh: GitHub CLI.
     sudo apt update
     sudo apt install -y gh
   - Authenticate and install extension:
     gh auth login
     gh extension install github/gh-copilot
   - alias setup:
     gh copilot alias -- zsh > ~/.gh-copilot-aliases.zsh
   - `.zshrc.local` sources `~/.gh-copilot-aliases.zsh` if present.

25. Claude Code config (optional):
   - ⚠️ BACKUP: Create backup of existing claude config:
     cp -r ~/.claude ~/.claude.bak.$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S) 2>/dev/null || true
   - Link claude files:
     ln -sfn ~/access-UK/claude/settings.json ~/.claude/settings.json
     ln -sfn ~/access-UK/claude/CLAUDE.md ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md

27. Verify symlinks:
=> $ pwd
/home/youruser
=> $ ls -lart ~ | grep access
lrwxrwxrwx ... access-UK -> ~/access-UK
lrwxrwxrwx ... .bashrc -> ~/.bashrc
lrwxrwxrwx ... .zshrc -> ~/.zshrc
lrwxrwxrwx ... .gitconfig -> ~/.gitconfig
lrwxrwxrwx ... .tmux.conf -> ~/.tmux.conf

28. Azure + C# + Go setup (optional):
   - Azure CLI: Azure resource and subscription operations.
     curl -sL https://aka.ms/InstallAzureCLIDeb | sudo bash
   - azd: Azure Developer CLI for app+infra bootstrap.
     curl -fsSL https://aka.ms/install-azd.sh | bash

   - .NET SDK (C# build/runtime toolchain):
     wget https://packages.microsoft.com/config/ubuntu/24.04/\
packages-microsoft-prod.deb -O packages-microsoft-prod.deb
     sudo dpkg -i packages-microsoft-prod.deb
     rm packages-microsoft-prod.deb
     sudo apt update
     sudo apt install -y dotnet-sdk-8.0
     dotnet --info
     dotnet --list-sdks

   - Go toolchain:
     sudo apt update
     sudo apt install -y golang-go
     go env GOPATH GOMODCACHE GOCACHE

   - Go language tools for editors:
     go install golang.org/x/tools/gopls@latest
     go install github.com/go-delve/delve/cmd/dlv@latest
     go install github.com/golangci/golangci-lint/cmd/golangci-lint@latest

   - Kubernetes tools (if needed for networking workflows):
     sudo apt update
     sudo apt install -y kubectl
     curl -fsSL \
https://raw.githubusercontent.com/helm/helm/main/scripts/get-helm-3 | bash

   - Azure login (optional - skip if not using Azure):
     # az login
     # az account set --subscription <your-subscription>
     # az account show
     echo "✓ Azure tools installed. Run 'az login' if you need Azure access."

zsh README details:
   - Additional setup steps from [zsh/README](zsh/README) for reference.
   - Git completion:
     cd ~/software
     curl -L https://github.com/git/git/raw/master/contrib/completion/\
git-completion.zsh -OL
     cd ~/
     ln -sfn ~/software/git-completion.zsh git-completion.zsh
   - Autojump (command directory navigation):
     - Installation:
       cd ~/software
       git clone https://github.com/wting/autojump.git
       cd autojump
       ./install.py
     - This installs autojump to ~/.autojump/etc/profile.d/autojump.sh
     - zshrc already sources this file if installed.
   - Git binary verification for zsh-git-prompt:
     - If you experience git status issues in your prompt:
       cd ~/access-UK/zsh/zsh-git-prompt
     - Edit `gitstatus.py` and verify line with git binary path.
     - Ensure it matches the output of `type git` or `which git`.
     - Example: `gitbinary='/usr/bin/git'` if `which git` returns
       `/usr/bin/git`.

Items intentionally skipped from old README (WSL not required):
- Old CentOS/yum/epel specific commands.
- Parcellite clipboard manager on Linux desktop.
- Samba server setup inside WSL.
- VNC server and full ubuntu-desktop setup inside WSL.
